From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 19:08:19 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] fix(signs): wrap Bedrock sign text like the native client

Bedrock wraps a long sign line into the following rows. Java clips
each line at the sign's width, so the end of every long Bedrock sign
line was missing and the lines below it sat too high.

Native 1.26.60.5 evidence from the Windows client:

- The sign renderer (0x146a7f670) passes the face's text to the
  layout at 0x140e6c950 with a budget of four rows. The layout
  (0x140e69dd0) splits the text into words, spaces and line breaks.
  A word that does not fit starts a new row. A space that does not
  fit stays at the end of its row. All lines share the four rows, so
  a wrapped line pushes later lines down and the last ones drop out.
- A word as wide as the row fills the current row and continues on
  the next rows (0x140e6b010). The head is the longest prefix that
  fits, found by dropping one character at a time but keeping one
  (0x140e6c4c0). If the break is inside a word, the layout measures
  again with room for a hyphen and appends "-".
- 0x140e6bbc0 skips formatting codes and adds no hyphen after a
  space, beside a hyphen, or beside wide script (Hangul Jamo,
  U+2329-232A, U+2E80-A4CF, U+AC00-D7FF, U+F900-FAFF, U+FE10-FE6F,
  U+FF01-FFEF, U+1B000-1B16F and U+20000-323AF). If the hyphenated
  break does not need a hyphen, the plain break is used.
- Rows are 89 units wide on standing signs and 60 on hanging signs.
  Java uses 90 and 60.

On Bedrock connections, the sign renderer now lays out each face this
way with the client font, which holds the converted Bedrock bitmap
metrics. The layout works on styled characters, so a hyphen takes the
style of the character before it. The rows go through Java's own
render cache in SignText, so the layout runs once per sign text.
Java servers keep Java's clipping.

Without PersistFormatting, the native row callback (0x146a94fe0)
adds each row as it is, and the renderer draws every row with its own
text call. A wrapped row therefore loses the formatting of the rows
before it, but keeps a formatting code that moved into it. With the
flag, the callback prefixes the codes that 0x1407f8260 collects until
a reset code. ViaBedrock marks faces without the flag with
"viabedrock:persist_formatting": false, which the add-on keeps on the
loaded SignText. Rows that continue a wrapped line on such faces start
from the line's base style until the next styled part.

